```

**Do not read `regress/artifacts/`, `tools/pipeline/ledger.json`, or `CHANGELOG.md`.** Cite anything
outside `GXEMUL-SEC` by absolute path.

Ordinary test-coverage question about an open-source emulator written in C99. Three unrelated
2021-vintage copies of `src/` exist elsewhere on this machine and would give line numbers off by
hundreds; the tree under review is the one above.

**Please check the premises rather than confirming them** — a recent review here had seven of nine
readers confirm a premise stated wrongly, and two of my own premises have already been refuted today.

## §1 — THE SITE

`src/devices/dev_sgi_ip32.c`, `DEVICE_ACCESS(crime)`. The access-width dispatch is, in structure:

```c
	if (len == 8) {
		...              /*  full 64-bit register access  */
	} else if (len == 4) {
		if (writeflag == MEM_WRITE) {
			...          /*  merge the 32-bit half  */
		} else {
			odata = d->reg[relative_addr / 8];
			if (relative_addr & 4)
				odata = (int32_t)odata;
			else
				odata = (int32_t)(odata >> 32);
		}
	} else {
		fatal("crime access len = %i!\n", len);
		exit(1);
	}

	switch (relative_addr) {
	...
```

There is no other width arm, so **every other access length — 1, 2, and anything wider — ends the
process.** `machine_sgi.c` installs this at `0x14000000` on the IP32 machines; CRIME is the memory and
interrupt controller, i.e. one of the first things any IP32 guest touches.

A design review has already settled the disposition: **decline with a bounded, latched announce**, not
a bus fault. (FAULT is mechanically available here because this is MIPS, and it was ruled out anyway:
this is a *present* device refusing an unsupported access width, not an absent one.)

## §2 — WHAT I NEED, AND IT IS NOT THE FIX

This project will not change what it cannot reproduce, and a reproduction must run a real guest access
through real address decode on a **committed, unmodified** machine description. Adding a machine or a
device in order to reach the site is disqualifying.

1. **Which machines install this device, and at what address?** Confirm or correct my `0x14000000`
   and my claim that the IP32 machines install it unconditionally.
2. **Can an ordinary guest load of one byte reach this handler with `len == 1`?** Trace it: does the
   memory dispatch pass the guest's access width through to the device callback unchanged, or does
   something upstream widen, split, or align it first? **This is the premise I am least sure of** —
   if the dispatcher never delivers `len == 1` to a device, the site is unreachable by that route and
   I want to know before a round is built on it.
3. **Is there any width restriction advertised** — in the device registration, its flags, or the
   machine setup — that a guest could be expected to respect?
4. **What existing vehicle in `regress/*.py` could drive this?** I know this tree has cold-debugger
   probes that plant guest instructions and step them. **Does anything already drive a MIPS guest on
   an IP32 machine?** Name what exists; a clear negative is as useful to me as a positive.
5. **What is the oracle?** If the fix declines instead of terminating, what does a correct
   implementation do that a wrong one does not — and specifically, what distinguishes it from
   (a) a handler that returns zero for every width, (b) one that silently services the byte access
   against the wrong register, and (c) a test where the access never happened at all? I want the
   failures a naive "it survived" assertion cannot see.
6. **How many other terminating calls are in this same file**, and would one test vehicle reach
   several? I am not asking for their repairs — only whether the coverage work groups.

Answer in full. Do not write any file; return the whole answer and I will save it.

